AB  - Federated Learning (FL) enables privacy-preserving training across AIoT devices, but faces challenges due to device heterogeneity, imbalanced data, and varying energy constraints. To address these challenges, we propose FL-LLM, a novel federated learning framework built upon a layer-wise aggregation scheme, which enables collaborative training across heterogeneous devices by assigning them adaptable model sizes. FL-LLM deploys a large language model (LLM)-based device selector on the server, which interprets uploaded device state information, adapts to task-specific requirements via lightweight fine-tuning, and generates effective decisions for device selection and model assignment. Extensive experiments on the CIFAR-10, SVHN, and Fashion-MNIST datasets demonstrate that FL-LLM significantly outperforms existing hierarchical FL methods, achieving an average accuracy improvement of 7.86% while reducing energy consumption by 17.95%. Moreover, compared to DRL-based methods, the LLM-based selector further enhances federated training accuracy by an average of 3.02%.
