AB  - The rapid emergence of generative artificial intelligence has fundamentally reshaped semantic representation in healthcare informatics. Traditionally, clinical semantics relied on symbolic knowledge structures — ontologies, controlled vocabularies, and rule-based mappings — to encode clinical meaning and ensure interoperability across health information systems. Large Language Models (LLMs) introduce a complementary paradigm in which semantic meaning is encoded within high-dimensional probabilistic embedding spaces learned from vast biomedical and natural language corpora. This chapter examines the role of LLMs and Small Language Models (SLMs) in capturing, interpreting, and generating clinically meaningful information from heterogeneous healthcare data, tracing their evolution from passive text generators into cognitive assistants capable of supporting clinical decision-making, diagnostic reasoning, and patient engagement. The chapter details the mechanics of Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) as a principled solution to model hallucinations, grounding AI outputs in verifiable, continuously updated medical literature. A layered defense-in-depth strategy for hallucination mitigation is presented, encompassing self-reflection, ensemble cross-checking, and ontology-grounded knowledge graph guarding. Prompt engineering is examined as a tool for constraining probabilistic models toward structured, guideline-adherent clinical reasoning, and the strategic trade-offs between fine-tuning and RAG for domain adaptation are discussed. Adversarial threats such as prompt injection and data exfiltration are cataloged within a broader discussion of regulatory compliance — spanning HIPAA, the EU AI Act, and India's DPDP Act — and ethical deployment considerations, including algorithmic bias and transparency. The chapter concludes by examining agentic clinical workflows as the emerging frontier of clinical AI, where multi-step reasoning, evidence retrieval, and human oversight converge to deliver safer, more reliable healthcare systems.
