AB  - Large language model (LLM)-based edge intelligence enables LLM services to run closer to users and data sources, reducing latency, bandwidth consumption, and privacy risks in a cloud–edge-client architecture. In such deployments, edge-hosted LLMs are often adapted with local enterprise or user data, such as customer records in financial services, which may later require removal to satisfy privacy regulations or user requests. However, simple storage deletion is insufficient as LLMs may still reveal memorized protected facts. Full retraining can remove such information but is too expensive for resource-constrained edge environments, while existing lightweight unlearning methods are often vulnerable to adversarial prompts that recover forgotten facts. To address these challenges, we propose Attack-Aware Preference Optimization (A2PO), a localized privacy unlearning framework for LLM-based edge intelligence. A2PO improves data removal robustness through three coordinated steps: attack-aware query augmentation, privacy-preserving response rewriting, and reference-anchored adapter preference optimization. This localized design eliminates cloud privacy data transmission and latency-intensive inference filters. Experiments on the TOFU benchmark with Llama-2-7B-chat and Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct show A2PO achieves forgetting F-scores of 0.89 and 0.95, respectively, preserving model utility and generation quality. Furthermore, A2PO provides stable adversarial protection over baselines and reduces update time by around 70% compared to full retraining, enabling efficient and privacy-preserving edge intelligence.
ER  - 

