AB  - Conventional fuzzing techniques are hampered by a fundamental semantic gap, limiting their ability to generate inputs that can penetrate complex program logic and discover deep vulnerabilities. The recent advent of Large Language Models (LLM), with their sophisticated code understanding and generation capabilities, offers a paradigm shift to overcome this limitation, ushering in the new field of LLM-driven Fuzzing. To structure this burgeoning area, we present a comprehensive survey of LLM-driven fuzzing, covering 47 representative studies from 2022 to the present. We introduce a novel taxonomy that organizes the field by the role LLM play in the fuzzing lifecycle – namely test case generation and feedback loop optimization – and systematically review existing works, from fine-tuning and prompt engineering to Fusion strategies. Quantitatively, Prompt Engineering accounts for over one-third of surveyed methods, and Fusion approaches constitute another substantial portion, reflecting a field-wide shift toward training-free LLM utilization. Our analysis synthesizes the state of the art and identifies key challenges at the intersection of Artificial Intelligence (AI) and security, including the inherent tension between LLM inference latency and fuzzing throughput, the conflict between the semantic fidelity of LLM and the adversarial nature of fuzzing, and the granularity mismatch in feedback mechanisms. Finally, we chart a roadmap for future research, advocating for specialized lightweight models, multi-modal context fusion, and adaptive, intelligent fuzzing frameworks to accelerate innovation. This work provides a crucial knowledge map for researchers and practitioners aiming to advance this new frontier.
