AB  - As large language models (LLMs) increasingly power REST APIs, LLM agents face security threats in API orchestration scenarios. Existing prompt injection research has focused on jailbreaking during text generation, lacking systematic consideration of state dependencies and role-based access control (RBAC) in API contexts. This paper presents a large-scale empirical study on Prompt-to-API-Call (P2A) injection attacks. Grounded in the “confused deputy” risk of agentic LLM architectures, we propose a standardized attack vector generation framework that extracts 14 fundamental malicious intents and constructs 52 generalized attack vectors across 5 categories (unrestricted, restricted-direct, restricted-indirect, injection-location, and obfuscation-bypass) via heuristic mutation. These vectors are adapted to 5 real-world heterogeneous backend systems, yielding 260 end-to-end evaluation scenarios. Based on 1040 baseline experiments across 4 representative open-source LLMs, the average attack success rate (ASR) reaches 59.7% under defense-free configuration, with unrestricted attacks achieving 84.5%. Cross-model analysis reveals that code-specialized models exhibit resistance to restricted-direct attacks (ASR: 40.0%) but limited defense against indirect injection and obfuscation-bypass attacks. Cross-system analysis demonstrates that server-side RBAC granularity determines the security lower bound, with deep RBAC architectures suppressing indirect injection ASR to 2.5%. To address limitations of conventional defenses, we propose the D5 Semantic Intent Validator, a dual-layer architecture combining deterministic rule-based pre-screening with guard-LLM semantic matching. Under the non-adaptive threat model, D5 achieves a residual ASR of 21.9% (a 67.4% relative reduction), the lowest aggregate ASR among the five evaluated defenses. The separate adaptive evaluation shows that mechanism-aware requests can bypass the semantic guard This work provides a standardized benchmark for evaluating LLM agent action-space security and evidence on the effectiveness and limitations of semantic intent validation.
