AB  - Large language model (LLM) has the potential to support cyber-physical decision-making by helping human operators interpret complex system conditions, especially during high-stress events with cognitive overload such as wildfire-driven disruptions. However, bringing LLMs into these environments also introduces new security concerns that traditional cyber-physical system (CPS) frameworks do not fully address. In particular, adversarial manipulation at the information technology (IT) layer can influence LLM reasoning, which may then shape human decisions and indirectly affect operational technology (OT) outcomes, even when the underlying physical system remains uncompromised. This paper explores this risk by first introducing the situationally aware assistant platform (SAAP) for cyber-physical energy systems, an LLM-augmented cyber-physical decision-support system in which the LLM operates as a passive, query-driven analytical assistant. Within this architecture, attack vectors are positioned using the MITRE adversarial threat landscape for artificial intelligence systems (ATLAS), allowing adversarial risks to be understood within an established artificial intelligence (AI) security framework. Guided by this, this paper models two representative inference time attacks, prompt injection and data manipulation, and examines how they can affect LLM behavior, response credibility, and downstream decision-support outcomes. Beyond deliberate attacks, this paper considers intrinsic vulnerabilities relevant to LLM-assisted decision support, including hallucination and human-related risks such as automation bias. To address these risks, it proposes a lightweight detection and mitigation framework combining user prompt screening, inference-time data integrity verification, and response-level credibility assessment, designed to identify adversarial and reliability risks while limiting their downstream operational impact through human-in-the-loop decision-making. In addition, the proposed independent per-query interaction design is intended to reduce susceptibility to context degradation, conversational drift, and adversarial context persistence across interactions. Experimental evaluation using an LLM, a physics-based wildfire simulation for electric energy systems, and associated cyber-system modeling suggests that the proposed approach effectively identifies adversarial influence, supports mitigation of reliability risks associated with LLM-assisted decision support, and limits potential IT-to-OT propagation. Overall, these findings demonstrate how such detection and mitigation strategies can support more secure and resilient integration of LLM-based reasoning components into safety-critical cyber-physical decision-support environments.
