AB  - Using large language models (LLMs) to process resumes and assess applicants creates a security risk: applicant-submitted documents may contain prompt injections that influence downstream models. There has been limited research on resume-based prompt injection and many defenses have been developed for conversational or general contexts. To address these challenges, we present a lightweight and training-free semantic detection layer called GUARD (Generative Use-case Adversarial Resume Detector) that detects resumes by comparing the similarity between the chunks of text present in the resume and a set of known attack templates before the text is passed to a downstream LLM. GUARD embeds chunks of resumes and reference attack templates in a shared sentence embedding space and ranks each resume based on the highest possible cosine similarity for any chunk–template pair. A semantic-component split divides the attack templates into reference and held-out attack templates to the extent that they contain an overlap in near paraphrases. With the primary fine-grained configuration, GUARD achieves a held-out AUC of 0.682 (95% CI [0.640,0.723]), compared with 0.794 (95% CI [0.771,0.817]) on the reference split, indicating bounded out-of-template discrimination. False negatives were mainly attributed to chunk dilution and insufficient coverage of reference attack library. GUARD reaches an AUC of 0.799 (95% CI [0.765, 0.833]) and recall of 0.438 with the frozen threshold of 0.70 over the 648 English CVs from the independently sourced recruitment dataset Djinni, at which point the attack injection is performed. The held-out AUC point-estimate (0.682) was higher than the four open-weight baselines with scores limited to near-zero values across both classes or saturation under maximum over chunks aggregation (0.654). GUARD is intended to be used as an extra security measure, and not to reject candidates automatically; the alert stream is intended for human review.
