AB  - Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) introduce profound security risks in open-world deployments, particularly cross-modal prompt injection attacks (PIAs), where adversaries embed malicious payloads within non-textual inputs such as images and graph-structured prompts to deviate systems from intended instructions. Current defenses, largely predicated on unimodal heuristics, frequently fail to generalize against these compositional and transferable threats. To overcome these limitations, we propose Cross-Modal Prompt Injection Unified Modeling and Game-Theoretic Defense Strategies (CM-PIUG), a framework integrating unified attack-surface modeling with game-theoretic optimization. Specifically, the framework formalizes the attack surface as a Unified Attack Surface Graph and utilizes contextual semantic-equivalence reduction to map heterogeneous carriers—spanning OCR-extracted text, native text, and graph prompts—into normalized semantic units. This mechanism effectively mitigates representational inconsistencies induced by modality differences. Furthermore, we characterize the interaction between the defender and extensive populations of attack variants as a Stackelberg mean-field game (SMFE). This formulation transforms the high-dimensional strategy space into a tractable “individual-distribution” structure, enabling the offline generation of near-equilibrium defenses suitable for rapid online matching. Empirical evaluations across text, vision-language, and graph prompt-learning benchmarks confirm that CM-PIUG consistently minimizes the attack success rate (ASR), enhances zero-shot detection and robustness over state-of-the-art baselines, and maintains minimal online overhead, thereby validating its scalability for real-world MLLM services.
