AB  - Large language models (LLMs) have shown promise for network traffic analysis, but their practical deployment is constrained by two key factors: the cost of vocabulary expansion for traffic-specific tokenization and the latency of full-depth inference in large models. To address these issues, we propose LightTrafficLLM, an efficient framework for malicious encrypted traffic detection. The framework consists of three components: a Vocabulary-Preserving Cross-Tokenizer Alignment and Compression (CTAC) mechanism that compresses traffic context without expanding the native LLM vocabulary; a structural pruning and knowledge distillation strategy that transfers task-specific capability from a 28-layer teacher to a lightweight 7-layer student; and a Brittleness-Aware Adaptive Inference (BAAI) module that selectively applies repeated voting only to uncertain samples. Experiments on five public benchmark datasets show that CTAC achieves an average token alignment ratio of 99.89% and reduces input length by 21.03% on average. Compared with the original TrafficLLM, LightTrafficLLM reduces model parameters by 71.5% and achieves an overall inference speedup of 3.78×–5.85×. Across the five datasets, LightTrafficLLM achieves three-run average weighted-F1 scores ranging from 0.945 to 0.990, with four datasets exceeding 0.98, indicating that substantial efficiency gains can be achieved while maintaining competitive detection performance.
