KW  - Large language models
KW  - Jailbreak defense
KW  - Semantic detection
AB  - Despite recent advances in alignment training, large language models (LLMs) remain vulnerable to black-box jailbreak attacks that exploit semantic ambiguity, role-playing prompts, and multi-turn prompt manipulation. Existing defenses mainly rely on static system prompts, decoding-time heuristics, or internal-model signals, which limit their robustness under distribution shifts and hinder deployment on closed-source APIs. To address these limitations, we propose Semantic Rollback Defense (SRD), a model-agnostic framework that reframes jailbreak mitigation as semantic intent recovery. SRD combines dense risk representations with a recursive intent rollback mechanism to identify and neutralize hidden malicious intent in adversarial prompts without requiring access to model internals. Unlike surface-level filtering approaches, SRD dynamically adapts safety constraints based on the recovered user intent, enabling targeted interventions while reducing unnecessary blocking. We evaluate SRD extensively across multiple open- and closed-source LLMs under diverse black-box jailbreak scenarios drawn from AdvBench and MT-Bench. SRD achieves an average defense success rate of 98.27%, outperforming several state-of-the-art baselines. These findings suggest that semantic-intent-based defense offers a practical and generalizable approach for improving LLM safety.
