AB  - The widespread application of Large Language Models (LLMs) has brought unprecedented opportunities; however, their rapid development has also exposed severe privacy leakage risks. Although existing surveys have preliminarily explored the privacy issues of LLMs, most lack a systematic deconstruction of multiple attack types under a unified threat model, nor have they established a fine-grained classification system to reveal their intrinsic correlations. To address this, this paper constructs a two-tier privacy attack classification framework, with adversary access rights (black-box, gray-box, white-box) as the primary classification and the core attack mechanism as the secondary classification. Within this framework, we systematically deconstruct five major attack paradigms: Membership Inference Attacks, Model Extraction Attacks, Prompt Extraction Attacks, Personally Identifiable Information Leakage Attacks, and Gradient Leakage Attacks. We provide an in-depth analysis of the core techniques and implementation paths for each attack type, evaluate their practical threats and limitations, and detail the corresponding defense strategies and their trade-offs among privacy, utility, and overhead.Through a comprehensive analysis of the latest advancements on both the attack and defense fronts, this paper delineates the advantages and bottlenecks of current technologies and proactively proposes key directions for future research. This study aims to provide researchers and practitioners with a comprehensive understanding of the LLM privacy landscape, serving as a reference for developing more secure LLMs.
