AB  - The integration of Large Language Models (LLMs) and Small Language Models (SLMs) as autonomous agents at the network edge marks a shift in cyber–physical systems and the Internet of Things (IoT). Deploying these non-deterministic reasoning engines in resource-constrained environments, however, disrupts traditional security paradigms. This survey reviews the threats to autonomous edge agents and their defenses. Through a layered taxonomy spanning the Perception, Cognition, Actuation, and Collaboration layers, we categorize the emerging cyber–physical threats, among them sensor-borne prompt injection, context-sequential injection, tool hijacking, and Sybil-based poisoning of the federated substrate. Several have been demonstrated on physical platforms, most starkly the jailbreaking of a deployed commercial robot, whereas the surveyed multi-agent attacks run only in simulation and software; attacks on quantized vision models further show that the aggressive quantization that edge hardware demands can open evasion vectors of its own. Evaluating the prevailing defenses, we expose the latency cost of inference-heavy semantic guardrails and the sequential blindness of stateless pre-execution firewalls, and we map their coverage against the taxonomy, separating experimental from inferred mappings. We flag stateful behavioral tracking as a largely unrefereed direction against the reasoning-to-action gap, not as a settled defense family. The survey’s principal output is a coverage map isolating three edge-induced threats that no edge-deployable runtime mechanism surveyed addresses: energy-targeted resource exhaustion, quantization-specific evasion, and model provenance against supply-chain weight poisoning. For the second, every surveyed hardening strategy that preserves adversarial robustness through quantization has been evaluated only on vision classifiers.
