AB  - Multilingual foundation Large Language Models (LLMs) have greatly improved accessibility for users across different regions, but they also introduce new surfaces for anomalous behavior. In this paper, we identify a new form of anomalous behavior in multilingual foundation LLMs, termed lingual-backdoor attack, in which the input language itself acts as the trigger and selectively induces attacker-desired abnormal outputs. Because language is naturally associated with specific speaker populations, lingual-backdoor enables precise targeting and may amplify social bias or misinformation risks. To study this threat, we first implement a task-specific baseline that injects the anomaly through conventional data poisoning on downstream tasks. Although effective in controlled settings, it generalizes poorly to realistic task-agnostic scenarios on instruction-tuned foundation LLMs. We therefore propose BadLingual, a task-agnostic lingual-backdoor that improves trigger robustness and cross-task generalization through PPL-constrained Greedy Coordinate Gradient-based Search (PGCG) and adversarial training. Extensive experiments across multiple models, languages, and downstream tasks show that BadLingual improves attack success rate by up to 37.35% over the baseline while preserving utility on clean inputs. Furthermore, widely used anomaly detection and recovery methods are largely ineffective against this language-triggered anomalous behavior. These findings reveal multilinguality as a previously underexplored source of anomalous behavior in foundation LLMs and highlight the urgent need for more effective anomaly-aware defenses.
