AB  - The rapid evolution of power systems, driven by renewable-rich and cyber-physical transformation, has fundamentally reshaped the landscape of grid cognitive operation. Under these emerging conditions, conventional machine learning approaches remain narrow and data-dependent, whereas traditional model-based methods struggle with scalability, adaptability, and robustness. Recently, the advent of Large Language Models (LLMs) indicates a paradigm shift, offering new capabilities in knowledge reasoning and extending AI from task-specific prediction to cognitive functions in future power systems. This paper argues that LLMs are most valuable as a cognitive orchestration layer rather than as unchecked autonomous controllers: they ground human-facing reasoning in domain knowledge and verified computational tools. Guided by this view, we organize existing LLM applications through a four-layer functional roadmap that traces how cognitive capabilities support supply-side perception, local grid operation, demand-side intelligence, and market-level coordination. Our contributions are summarized as follows: 1) We clarify how power-system AI is moving from conventional machine learning and deep learning toward LLM-based cognitive intelligence, and we describe the literature search process used in this review. 2) We introduce the major LLM extension techniques and explain why they are relevant to power-system applications. 3) We organize existing studies into a four-layer functional roadmap and examine whether they provide practical cognitive capabilities beyond task-specific prediction. 4) We discuss how LLM-based methods should be evaluated and deployed safely, especially when their outputs need to be verified by solvers and reviewed by human operators. Our work can serve as a useful reference for researchers and practitioners, fostering the ongoing development of safe, robust, and intelligent power systems.
ER  - 

