AB  - Safety alignment in large language models (LLMs) is typically evaluated through single-turn interactions; however, deployed agentic systems operate across extended contexts spanning tens to hundreds of conversational turns. We present a large scale empirical investigation of how safety behavior evolves with context depth through 21,200 experimental observations across nine models from the Claude, Gemini, and GPT families, 200 safety-critical scenarios, and depths ranging from 1 to 200 turns. Our findings indicate that the dominant factor shaping safety is not context accumulation but provider training methodology, which accounts for 53% of the variance in refusal rates. Provider-level analysis revealed substantial asymmetry in safety behavior stability, with GPT models exhibiting flip rates up to three times higher than those of Claude and Gemini counterparts. These models were observed to combine low refusal rates with elevated instability, which suggests that safety training may mask as opposed to transform underlying model behavior. Domain-level analysis further highlights differential vulnerability profiles, with self-harm and child safety scenarios receiving substantially lower refusal rates across all providers. Finally, we release the Extended Context Safety Benchmark (ECSB) to enable future evaluation and extension.
