AB  - Jailbreak attacks reveal a critical weakness in the safety alignment of current large language models: although aligned models may refuse harmful requests at the output level, they can still retain latent pathways associated with unsafe responses internally, and these pathways may be reactivated by adversarial prompts. In this paper, we propose Cross-layer Manifold Attack (CMA), which suppresses harmful-prompt components while reinforcing benign-prompt components in hidden states across multiple layers, progressively shifting the model’s internal representations from refusal-related regions toward compliance-related regions. We further observe that CMA-induced latent shifts exhibit directional patterns related to those produced by representative jailbreak attacks. Building on this observation, we propose Cross-layer Manifold Projection (CMP). Rather than treating jailbreak robustness solely as a behavioral classification problem, CMP formulates it as a problem of controlling internal representations. Specifically, CMP approximates harmful and benign activation structures using layer-wise low-rank subspaces obtained by truncated SVD and applies CMA-style perturbations during training to simulate jailbreak-like latent shifts. The model is then safety-tuned to maintain stable refusal behavior under these perturbed hidden states. Experiments across multiple model architectures show that CMP substantially improves robustness against both prompt-level and representation-level jailbreak attacks while largely preserving general capabilities. Compared with existing adversarial fine-tuning baselines, CMP also maintains a favorable over-refusal trade-off and shows strong robustness under diverse latent-space perturbations, suggesting that representation-aware training can improve safety robustness beyond standard output-level refusal supervision.
