AB  - Jailbreak prompts, crafted inputs that bypass an aligned language model’s refusals, are routinely blocked by prompt-level detectors deployed as guardrails in front of hosted LLM APIs. Existing detectors are typically fine-tuned transformers reported at F1-scores above 0.95, but these numbers usually come from random in-distribution splits of a single corpus. Behavior under realistic cross-source distribution shift therefore remains largely unverified. In this study, eight detector configurations spanning TF–IDF lexical baselines, four frozen-embedding classifiers, and the fine-tuned transformers BERT-base and ModernBERT are evaluated across five public jailbreak datasets under three protocols: in-distribution, pairwise source-held-out cross-dataset transfer, and leave-one-out mixed training. A pairwise contamination audit precedes the benchmark and excludes the two DAN↔jackhhao directional pairs, whose jailbreak classes overlap by construction, from the cross-dataset protocol. Among the configurations tested, a frozen MiniLM-L6 Sentence-BERT embedding with a class-balanced logistic-regression head is the strongest under source-held-out cross-dataset transfer, while remaining close to the fine-tuned transformers in-distribution (a mean jailbreak-class F1 of 0.913 vs 0.940 for BERT-base) and under leave-one-out mixed training (0.886 vs 0.897). On cross-dataset transfer, it outperforms BERT-base by +0.215 F1 on average (0.608 vs 0.393 over 18 directional pairs) and also exceeds the recent ModernBERT encoder (0.519). An equalized class-weighting ablation indicates this gap is not merely a reweighting artefact: an unweighted embedding classifier still leads BERT-base, and class-weighting BERT-base does not close the gap. An exploratory DAN/jackhhao contrast suggests non-shared dataset factors play a role in transfer, although they cannot be cleanly separated within this benchmark.
