AB  - System logs play a pivotal role in maintaining network infrastructure reliability and security. However, their sheer volume, diversity, and unstructured nature pose significant challenges for timely and effective analysis. Traditional methods increasingly face limitations in generalizability, interpretability, and dependence on large-scale labeled datasets. Large Language Models (LLMs) have emerged as promising tools for understanding, summarizing, and reasoning over complex logs. Recent studies show growing interest in applying LLMs to diverse log analysis tasks, reflecting a shift toward more intelligent and adaptable solutions. This survey comprehensively examines LLM applications in system log analysis. We first discuss the motivations for adopting LLMs in log-related tasks and categorize representative studies into five areas: log parsing, log enhancement, anomaly detection, root cause analysis (RCA), and emerging log tasks. We further analyze model types, technical benefits, practical limitations, and deployment risks. We introduce a structured cross-task comparison framework covering model strategy, log sources, evaluation focus, practical constraints, generalization, and reproducibility. We also include an exploratory LLM-assisted attitude annotation to examine how authors describe LLM suitability and limitations across log-analysis tasks. Finally, we summarize key challenges and future research directions. This work provides guidance for researchers and practitioners seeking to advance log analysis through LLM technologies.
