AB  - Integrating large language models (LLMs) into the learning management systems (LMSs) has transformational potential when it comes to personalized learning options, automatic tutoring, and content generation. However, this integration introduces significant security and privacy challenges, including data leakage, prompt injection, and unauthorized access. These issues have been discussed in this study, and it has offered a general integrative plan to ensure secure and privacy-friendly mainstreaming of LLMs in LMSs. The primary implementation priorities include using privacy-preserving techniques, like differential privacy and federated learning, the presence of high-level access controls, managing APIs and plugins, and real-time monitoring of suspicious activities. In addition to that, the chapter also depicts compliance with the regulation of data protection, such as the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), the Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act (FERPA), and the ethical implications of AI-based content creation in the educational context. A case study scenario has been used to describe the proposed framework in which it has been used in a university LMS environment due to which the increase in security and postgraduate privacy had been found to be effective. It can be concluded in the chapter that the most beneficial strategies can be observed as supporting the fact that it is important to promote the harmonization of functionality and security in artificial intelligence within the field of education, and by pointing out the importance of conducting further research dedicated to the issues that appear in the field of developing artificial intelligence.
